## **Time Complexity (TC) and Space Complexity (SC) – Queue Using Two Stacks**

**1. Enqueue (Insertion)**

- **TC:** O(1) – Element is directly inserted into stack `s1`.
- **SC:** O(1) auxiliary space – No extra data structure is used.

**2. Dequeue (Deletion)**

- **Best Case TC:** O(1) – If `s2` is not empty, simply remove its top element.
- **Worst Case TC:** O(N) – If `s2` is empty, all elements from `s1` must be transferred to `s2`.
- **Amortized TC:** O(1) – Each element is transferred from `s1` to `s2` at most once.
- **SC:** O(1) auxiliary space – Uses the existing stacks for transferring elements.

**3. Peek (Front Element)**

- **Best Case TC:** O(1) – If `s2` is not empty, directly access its top element.
- **Worst Case TC:** O(N) – If `s2` is empty, all elements from `s1` must be transferred to `s2`.
- **Amortized TC:** O(1) – Each element is transferred at most once.
- **SC:** O(1) auxiliary space – No additional data structure is created.

**4. isEmpty (Check Queue Empty)**

- **TC:** O(1) – Checks whether both stacks are empty.
- **SC:** O(1) – Uses constant extra space.

**5. Display**

- **TC:** O(N) – Traverses all elements to display them.
- **SC:** O(N) – Creates temporary copies of stacks and an additional temporary stack.

### **Overall Complexity**

- **Time Complexity:** O(1) amortized for enqueue, dequeue and peek.
- **Space Complexity:** O(N) for storing N elements in the queue.
- **Auxiliary Space:** O(N) for the display operation; O(1) for other operations.

*Note: Dequeue and peek have O(N) worst-case time complexity but O(1) amortized time complexity.*



Amortized analysis does not mean every operation takes O(1). It means that expensive operations happen infrequently enough that the average cost over a sequence of operations is O(1).



#include <iostream>
#include <stack>
using namespace std;

class Queue {
private:
    stack<int> s1, s2;

public:
    // Enqueue operation
    void enqueue(int x) {
        s1.push(x);
        cout << x << " inserted into queue\n";
    }

    // Dequeue operation
    void dequeue() {
        if (s1.empty() && s2.empty()) {
            cout << "Queue Underflow\n";
            return;
        }

        if (s2.empty()) {
            while (!s1.empty()) {
                s2.push(s1.top());
                s1.pop();
            }
        }

        cout << s2.top() << " removed from queue\n";
        s2.pop();
    }

    // Peek operation
    void peek() {
        if (s1.empty() && s2.empty()) {
            cout << "Queue is empty\n";
            return;
        }

        if (s2.empty()) {
            while (!s1.empty()) {
                s2.push(s1.top());
                s1.pop();
            }
        }

        cout << "Front element: " << s2.top() << endl;
    }

    // Check if queue is empty
    bool isEmpty() {
        return s1.empty() && s2.empty();
    }

    // Display queue
    void display() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            cout << "Queue is empty\n";
            return;
        }

        stack<int> t1 = s1, t2 = s2;

        cout << "Queue elements: ";

        // Elements already in s2 are in correct order
        while (!t2.empty()) {
            cout << t2.top() << " ";
            t2.pop();
        }

        // Reverse s1 to print in queue order
        stack<int> temp;
        while (!t1.empty()) {
            temp.push(t1.top());
            t1.pop();
        }

        while (!temp.empty()) {
            cout << temp.top() << " ";
            temp.pop();
        }

        cout << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Queue q;

    q.enqueue(10);
    q.enqueue(20);
    q.enqueue(30);

    q.display();

    q.peek();

    q.dequeue();

    q.display();

    return 0;
}
